# Semantic commits and pull request titles

Every commit subject and every pull request title uses [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/).

```
<type>(<optional-scope>): <imperative description>
```

## Types

| Type | Use for |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | A user-facing feature |
| `fix` | A bug fix |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `style` | Formatting only, no behavior change |
| `refactor` | Restructure with no behavior change |
| `perf` | A performance improvement |
| `test` | Tests only |
| `build` | Dependencies or the build |
| `ci` | CI configuration |
| `chore` | Maintenance that is none of the above |
| `revert` | Reverting a previous commit |

## Rules

- Lowercase type. Optional scope in parentheses, lowercase (`feat(checkout): …`).
- Imperative description, no trailing period, under 72 characters.
- Blank line before a body or footer. Put issue links in the footer (`Fixes #123`), not in the subject.
- Pull request titles use the same subject line. The PR body still carries `Fixes #<N>` or `Refs #<N>`.
- One concern per commit. A breaking change adds `!` after the type or scope (`feat!: …`) and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.

```
feat(checkout): require a configurable minimum order amount

Fixes #403
```

```
fix: drop the extra limit validation on additional groups
```
